 Light Propagation Volume (LPV), specifically the Opacity Propagation Volume (OPV) variant, is a real-time method for precomputing the optical depth $T(S,X)$ from each light source $S$ to each cell $X$ in a 3D grid. It solves the problem of efficiently calculating light attenuation (extinction) through heterogeneous participating media by propagating density information radially from light sources through a 3D volume on the GPU. Unlike standard radiance propagation which suffers from rapid scattering errors in dense media, OPV focuses on accurate optical depth integration, enabling correct shadowing and attenuation for subsequent ray-marched rendering. It builds on [Ray-marched Volumetric Lighting with Density Thresholding](2456-ray-marched-volumetric-lighting-with-density-thresholding.md) by providing the precomputed attenuation data required for that rendering step.
 
+### Volumetric Lighting (Cheat Method)
+An approach using cone-shaped geometry matching the light source, combined with translucency and the Fresnel effect to soften edges [S3, unofficial]. It is computationally inexpensive to render but comes with limitations: the effect disappears when the viewer is inside the volume, does not hold up from all angles, and cannot account for light occlusion (e.g., shadows) [S3, unofficial].
